				<div id="mw-content-text" lang="en" dir="ltr" class="mw-content-ltr"><div class="mw-parser-output"><div class="thumb tright"><div class="thumbinner" style="width:302px;"><a href="/wiki/index.php/File:MD_SpatialRepresentation.png" class="image"><img alt="" src="/wiki/images/thumb/4/41/MD_SpatialRepresentation.png/300px-MD_SpatialRepresentation.png" width="300" height="225" class="thumbimage" srcset="/wiki/images/thumb/4/41/MD_SpatialRepresentation.png/450px-MD_SpatialRepresentation.png 1.5x, /wiki/images/thumb/4/41/MD_SpatialRepresentation.png/600px-MD_SpatialRepresentation.png 2x" /></a>  <div class="thumbcaption"><div class="magnify"><a href="/wiki/index.php/File:MD_SpatialRepresentation.png" class="internal" title="Enlarge"></a></div>MD_SpatialRepresentation</div></div></div>
<p>Describing the spatial representation of a dataset is very important for geospatial data. The ISO MD_SpatialRepresentation Object offers several choices. These choices are represented by <a rel="nofollow" class="external text" href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glossary_of_Unified_Modeling_Language_terms">Abstract Objects</a> in the UML. They can be instantiated in several ways. The MD_SpatialRepresentation Object is abstract, it is implemented either as a MD_GridSpatialRepresentation Object (on the left side of the Figure) or as a MD_VectorSpatialRepresentation Object on the right.
</p>
